include includes/folio-shared.pug

html(lang="en")
    head
        +folioHead('A-to-z First Claim: sellers were unsure what to do next | Aaron Benjamin', 'I redesigned the Amazon Seller Central first-claim email so a punitive A-to-z notice could teach sellers how to avoid the next claim.', '/a-to-z-first-claim.html', 'folio', '/assets/img/folio/project--a-to-z-first-claim/a-to-z-first-claim--hero-2x.jpg')
    body.folio.folio--case
        +folioShell

        +folioHeader('work', 'A-to-z First Claim', '/#a-to-z-first-claim')

        main#main.folio-main.folio-main--case
            article.folio-case
                header.folio-case__hero
                    figure.folio-case__hero-media
                        img(src="/assets/img/folio/project--a-to-z-first-claim/a-to-z-first-claim--hero-2x.jpg", alt="A seller at a home workspace reviewing the A-to-z claims list in Amazon Seller Central on a laptop", width="1920" height="1320" fetchpriority="high")
                    .folio-case__intro
                        p.folio-eyebrow Amazon · A-to-z First Claim
                        h1 A warning could explain the penalty and still leave a seller lost.
                        p.folio-case__dek A first-claim email told sellers about a penalty without making the next action clear. I redesigned it to explain the stakes, first-claim coverage, and how to prevent another claim.
                    +caseFacts
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Role
                            dd UX Designer II, Amazon Seller Central
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Scope
                            dd Seller research, claims-process mapping, and first-claim email
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Key decision
                            dd Treat the first claim as a covered learning event, not only a defect
                        .folio-case__fact
                            dt Outcome
                            dd Reported higher seller confidence and lower A-to-z support-call volume after 60 days in the U.S.

                +caseSection({labelledby: "atoz-support-title"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow I — Inspiration
                    h2#atoz-support-title Support contacts showed what sellers did not understand
                    p The first-claim notification arrived at a difficult moment: a customer had been refunded, an account metric was at stake, and the seller needed to decide what to do. Explaining that something had gone wrong was only the beginning. The message also needed to make the next action understandable.
                    p When a customer’s order from a third-party seller goes wrong and the seller does not respond in time, Amazon steps in. The customer gets a refund. The seller gets an A-to-z claim, a hit to Order Defect Rate, and an email that often felt like discipline.
                    p I reviewed 250 Seller Support contacts tied to A-to-z. Appeals, disputes, and status questions accounted for a substantial share; the analysis marked many of them preventable. The opportunity was to explain the process before a confused seller needed support.

                +imageBrief({"role": "Context", "title": "A claim with no clear next step", "composition": "Show an abstract notification beside the seller questions: What happened? What is covered? What do I do next? No actual account or order data.", "purpose": "Establish the opening tension.", "caption": "Conceptual reconstruction of the uncertainty found in seller research.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "atoz-map-title", tint: true, prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow D — Design Process
                        h2#atoz-map-title Sellers needed an explanation, not another warning
                        p I mapped messaging, eligibility, disputes, and appeals with stakeholders and domain experts. Seller interviews showed that actions were unclear, Order Defect Rate mattered deeply, and sellers lacked confidence they could avoid the next claim.
                        p I aligned partners on three tenets: use the first claim as a learning opportunity, inform rather than discipline, and help sellers prevent a repeat. Those tenets changed the structure of the notification, not just its tone.
                    +storyFlow({id: "atoz-message-caption", title: "The four jobs of the first-claim email", nodes: [{label: "Explain", detail: "Show claim details and what happened."}, {label: "Set expectations", detail: "Describe A-to-z and Order Defect Rate."}, {label: "Cover", detail: "Explain Amazon’s first-claim refund coverage."}, {label: "Coach", detail: "Give practical steps to avoid another claim."}], caption: "The message moves from the current event to the seller’s next action."})


                +imageBrief({"role": "Process", "title": "The decision and its alternatives", "composition": "Compare a warning-led hierarchy with claim details, explanation, first-claim coverage, and prevention guidance. Label sample text as reconstructed.", "purpose": "Make the research-to-decision connection visible.", "caption": "A reconstructed comparison of the choices described in this story.", "ratio": "3:2", "type": "Labeled reconstruction"})
                +caseSection({labelledby: "atoz-engagement"})
                    p.folio-eyebrow E — Engagement
                    h2#atoz-engagement Give the program a shared teaching purpose
                    p I worked with stakeholders and domain experts to map how messaging, eligibility, disputes, and appeals fitted together. The notification sat inside a wider program, so an encouraging tone could not substitute for accurate expectations about coverage and Order Defect Rate.
                    p The three tenets gave partners a shared basis for evaluating the message: make the first claim a learning opportunity, inform rather than discipline, and help prevent a repeat. This connected the communication design to the seller interviews rather than treating it as a cosmetic rewrite.
                    p First-claim coverage was a program decision. I owned the notification design within that direction, while delivery depended on the wider team. Separating those responsibilities also matters when reading the reported outcomes: the results belong to the program, not an isolated test of my email.
                +caseSection({labelledby: "atoz-email-title", prose: false})
                    .folio-case__prose
                        p.folio-eyebrow A — Action
                        h2#atoz-email-title I rebuilt the email around what sellers needed to know
                        p The pivotal decision was to treat the first claim as a covered learning event. The email led with claim details, explained what an A-to-z claim is and how ODR works, stated that Amazon would cover the customer refund on the first claim, and listed concrete practices for avoiding another one. The stakes stayed visible. The tone changed.
                        p I owned the notification design. First-claim coverage was a program decision, and delivery depended on the wider team.
                    +caseFigure({labelledby: "atoz-email-caption", src: "/assets/img/folio/project--a-to-z-first-claim/a-to-z-first-claim--email-2x.png", alt: "First-claim email mock annotated with its four jobs: claim details, set expectations, cover the claim, and help the seller", width: "2220", height: "1826", loading: "lazy", caption: "Redesigned first-claim email, annotated with its four jobs: claim details up front, what happens next, first claim covered, and practices that prevent the next claim. Sample seller and order data.", captionId: "atoz-email-caption"})

                +caseOutcome("atoz-outcome-title", "After launch, sellers reported more confidence")
                    +caseResults([{value: "88%", label: "Of email openers reported confidence in avoiding future claims"}, {value: "14%", label: "Reported reduction in A-to-z-related support-call volume"}], "Source: project presentation, 60 days after the U.S. launch. Retrospective results have not been independently revalidated.")
                    p The presentation also reports seller-initiated refunds trending toward a 30% year-over-year increase. These are program-level results, not an isolated test of the email.
                    p My takeaway: first-claim coverage created room to teach. Specific explanations and prevention steps made that investment useful to the seller.

                +imageBrief({"role": "Outcome", "title": "What the evidence supports", "composition": "Use only the results stated in this case. Keep population, timeframe, qualitative status, and any attribution limits beside the result.", "purpose": "Resolve the story without overstating impact.", "caption": "Evidence summary reconstructed from the documented account; no invented measurements.", "ratio": "16:9", "type": "Evidence summary"})
            +folioFooter(false, 'I’m happy to walk through the research and notification decisions.', {href: '/curbside-pickup.html', label: 'Next: Curbside Pickup'})
